  "result": "Perfect! All 271 tests pass (including 24 new exportCsv tests). Here's what I've implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\n**`src/export.js`** \u2014 New file with CSV export functionality\n\n**`exportCsv(invoices)`** function:\n- Takes an array of invoice objects\n- Returns a CSV string with:\n  - **Header**: `number,customer,issueDate,dueDate,status,subtotal,discount,vat,total,due`\n  - **Data lines**: One per invoice in the order given\n  - **Customer name**: Looked up from customer ID\n  - **Amounts**: Plain euros with 2 decimals and a dot (e.g., `1234.50`)\n    - No currency sign\n    - No thousands separator\n  - **CSV escaping**:\n    - Fields containing comma or double quote are wrapped in double quotes\n    - Double quotes inside fields are escaped by doubling them\n  - **Line endings**: Each line ends with `\\n`\n\n**Helper functions**:\n- `formatEurosPlain(cents)` \u2014 Converts cents to euros format (123456 \u2192 \"1234.56\")\n- `escapeCsvField(value)` \u2014 Properly escapes CSV fields per RFC 4180\n\n**`test/export.test.js`** \u2014 24 comprehensive tests covering:\n- Empty arrays and single/multiple invoices\n- Different invoice statuses (draft, issued, partially_paid, paid)\n- Customer name lookup and escaping (including quoted names)\n- Amount formatting (small amounts, large amounts, various discounts)\n- CSV field escaping (commas, quotes)\n- Multiple VAT rates\n- Draft invoices (no number, but has calculated dueDate)\n- Paid invoices (zero due)\n- Order preservation\n- Trailing newlines\n- Edge cases (customer lookup failure, etc.)\n\nAll 271 tests pass successfully (247 previous + 24 export).",
